yoyo diff, yoyo commit and yoyo blame <file> now exit 1 when run outside a git repository. Before, they printed the error and exited 0. It's committed as 0d765e7a. Build, all tests, clippy with -D warnings and the format check pass.

What changed:
• Each command now has a version that returns its status: handle_diff_status, handle_commit_status and handle_blame_status. They return 1 when the "not in a git repository" error prints, and 0 otherwise. Nothing parses output text.
• The original functions just call the new ones and throw the code away, so the interactive /diff, /commit and /blame behave exactly as before.
• The three shell entry points in src/dispatch_sub.rs pass the code to slice 1's exit_if_nonzero. No new exit path was added.
• These still exit 0 on purpose: an empty diff, "nothing staged", blame usage errors, and other failures inside a repo (for example commit -m when the git commit itself fails).

Blame caveat: yoyo blame decides "not a repo" by reading git's error text. That check was already there and I didn't add it, but the new exit code depends on it, so a git version that words the error differently would exit 0 again.

Tests:
• Three existing tests dispatched these commands in-process, and the new exit call would kill the test binary if run from a non-git directory. I changed them to call the new functions and check for 0; none were deleted.
• New tests in tests/integration.rs run the real binary in a temp dir that isn't a repo. They check exit 1 and that the original error text is unchanged, for diff, commit and blame x.rs. That file is outside the 3-source-file budget, and the commit message says so.
• Near miss: yoyo diff in a fresh git init repo still exits 0.
• Positive control: making the diff not-a-repo branch return 0 failed git_subcommands_exit_one_outside_a_git_repository by name. Restoring it made the test pass again, and no NEUTERED marker is left.
• By hand from /tmp: yoyo diff and yoyo blame x.rs both exit 1.

The paragraph on exit codes in docs/src/usage/commands.md listed which subcommands report failure, so I added these three. I also commented on #982 with what this slice covered and what's left: slice 3 (lint, health, changelog, bare run), the other failures inside a repo, and the still-undecided find/grep no-match convention.
